[al:新概念英语（四）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:The Sculptor Speaks]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.61]Lesson 31 | 第31课
[00:02.76]The sculptor speaks | 雕塑家的语言
[00:10.95]What do you have to be able to do to appreciate sculpture? | 要欣赏雕塑，你必须能够做什么？
[00:17.35]Appreciation of sculpture depends upon the ability to respond to form in 3 dimensions. | 对雕塑的鉴赏力取决于对三维形体的反应能力。
[00:25.69]That is perhaps why sculpture has been described as the most difficult of all arts; | 这也许就是为什么雕塑被说成是一切艺术中最困难的艺术；
[00:32.10]certainly it is more difficult than the arts which involve appreciation of flat forms, shape in only two dimensions. | 它无疑比那些只涉及平面形式、只有二维形状的艺术更困难。
[00:41.91]Many more people are 'form-blind' than colour-blind. | “形盲”的人比色盲的人要多得多。
[00:48.46]The child learning to see, first distinguishes only two-dimensional shape; it cannot judge distances, depths. | 学着看东西的儿童起初只能分辨二维形状，不能判断距离和深度。
[00:57.88]Later, for its personal safety and practical needs, it has to develop (partly by means of touch) the ability to judge roughly 3-dimensonal distances. | 后来，为了自身的安全和实际需要，他必须发展（部分通过触觉）粗略判断三维距离的能力。
[01:11.81]But having satisfied the requirements of practical necessity, most people go no further. | 但是，在满足了实际需要之后，大多数人就到此为止了。
[01:18.94]Though they may attain considerable accuracy in the perception of flat form, | 尽管他们对平面形状的感知可以达到相当的准确程度，
[01:24.99]they do not make the further intellectual and emotional effort needed to comprehend form in its full spatial existence. | 他们却不再做出智力和情感上的进一步努力，去理解形体在完整空间中的存在。
[01:35.23]This is what the sculptor must do. | 而这正是雕塑家必须做到的。
[01:38.04]He must strive continually to think of and use, form in its full spatial completeness. | 他必须不断地努力，从形体的完整空间性去思考和运用它。
[01:46.88]He gets the solid shape as it were, inside his head--he thinks of it, whatever its size, as if he were holding it completely enclosed in the hollow of his hand. | 可以说，他把立体的形体装进了自己的头脑——他思考它时，不管它有多大，都仿佛完全把它握在自己手心里。
[02:00.70]He mentally visualizes a complex form from all round itself; | 他在头脑中从四面八方想像一个复杂的形体；
[02:07.30]he knows while he looks at one side what the other side is like; he identifies himself with its centre of gravity, its mass, its weight; | 他看着一个侧面时，知道另一个侧面是什么样子；他把自己等同于它的重心、它的质量、它的重量；
[02:18.34]he realizes its volume as the space that the shape displaces in the air. | 他把它的体积理解为形体在空气中所排开的空间。
[02:24.81]And the sensitive observer of sculpture must also learn to feel shape simply as shape, not as description or reminiscence. | 而雕塑的敏锐的观察者还必须学会把形体仅仅作为形体来感受，而不是作为描述或联想。
[02:34.47]He must, for example, perceive an egg as a simple single solid shape quite apart from its significance as food, | 例如，他必须把一只鸡蛋感受为一个简单的单一立体形状，而全然不管它作为食物的意义，
[02:44.43]or from the literary idea that it will become a bird. | 也不管它日后会变成鸟这种文字上的想法。
[02:49.08]And so with solids such as a shell, a nut, a plum, a pear, a tadpole, a mushroom, | 对于诸如贝壳、核桃、李子、梨、蝌蚪、蘑菇、
[02:58.09]a mountain peak, a kidney, a carrot, a tree-trunk, a bird, a bud, a lark, a ladybird, a bulrush, a bone. | 山峰、肾脏、胡萝卜、树干、鸟、花蕾、云雀、瓢虫、芦苇、骨头等立体物，也是如此。
[03:11.28]From these he can go on to appreciate more complex forms or combinations of several forms. | 从这些形体出发，他可以进而欣赏更复杂的形体或者几个形体的组合。
